;; Waveshare CM5-IO-WIRELESS-BASE.
;;
;; The industrial carrier: two isolated RS485 ports and an isolated CAN, all
;; on screw terminals with surge and ESD protection and selectable 120R
;; termination; an M.2 B-key for a 4G/5G or LoRa module with a Mini-PCIe
;; adapter in the box; an M.2 M-key for NVMe; gigabit Ethernet; 7-36V input;
;; and DIN-rail mounting. One Ethernet port, unlike the dual-ETH boards, so
;; this is the protocol-breadth carrier rather than the gateway one.
;;
;; UNVERIFIED: the device tree below is the official IO board's, which is
;; where a Waveshare carrier usually starts, and the vendor's wiki at
;; docs.waveshare.com/CM5-IO-WIRELESS-BASE is what settles it. The RS485 and
;; CAN lines almost certainly need device tree overlays of their own - the
;; RS485 ports are behind a UART expander and the CAN behind an MCP251x-class
;; controller - and those overlays belong in this fragment once the board is
;; in front of you. Until then this composes and boots; it does not yet claim
;; the serial ports work.

(fragment target:cm5-io-wireless
  (doc "Waveshare CM5-IO-WIRELESS-BASE: RS485, CAN, cellular, NVMe")

  (provides
    ;; The module's, claimed here because a carrier is only ever a board
    ;; with a module in it, and only a target may claim a capability.
    (capability compiler)
    (capability glibc)
    (capability mmu)

    (capability csi-camera)
    (capability sdcard)
    (capability serial-console)
    (capability nvme)
    (capability rs485)
    (capability can-bus)
    (capability cellular))

  (buildroot
    ;; The BCM2712's 4k page size fragment, stated by the target for the same
    ;; reason every Raspberry Pi target states it: silt writes its own kernel
    ;; fragment into this symbol, and only a target's value survives that.
    (value BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES "board/raspberrypi/linux-4k-page-size.fragment")
    (value BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_INTREE_DTS_NAME "broadcom/bcm2712-rpi-cm5-cm5io")))
